<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ndjamena"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ndjamena.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">N'Djamena</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">N'Djamena is the capital and largest city of Chad, at the confluence of the Chari and Logone rivers on the border with Cameroon.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/n-f-s-grundtvig"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/n-f-s-grundtvig.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">N. F. S. Grundtvig</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">N.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/na-atabia"><span class="enc-name">Na Atabia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Na Atabia was a ruler (nayiri) of Mamprugu, the oldest of the Mossi-Dagomba kingdoms in northern Ghana, around 1700.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/na-gbewaa"><span class="enc-name">Na Gbewaa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Na Gbewaa is the founding ancestor of the Mole-Dagbani kingdoms of northern Ghana in oral tradition, said to have settled at Pusiga, probably in the 14th or 15th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/na-nyagse"><span class="enc-name">Na Nyagse</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Na Nyagse was a ruler of Dagbon, in present-day northern Ghana, remembered in tradition as the king who created the kingdom in its lasting form, probably in the 15th…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/na-zangina"><span class="enc-name">Na Zangina</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Na Zangina was ya-na (king) of Dagbon in northern Ghana in the early 18th century and is remembered as the first king to adopt Islam at court.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/naaman-river"><span class="enc-name">Na'aman River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Na'aman is a short coastal river in northern Israel that flows into the Mediterranean just south of Acre.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/naab"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/naab.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Naab</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Naab is a river in the Upper Palatinate in Bavaria, Germany, and a left tributary of the Danube.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/naaba-kango"><span class="enc-name">Naaba Kango</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Naaba Kango was king of Yatenga, a Mossi kingdom in the north of present-day Burkina Faso, in the second half of the 18th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/nabadwip"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/nabadwip.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Nabadwip</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Nabadwip, also called Nadia, is a town in West Bengal, India, on the Bhagirathi river.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/nabeshima-clan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/nabeshima-clan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Nabeshima clan</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Nabeshima were a samurai family of Hizen province in Kyushu who ruled the Saga domain throughout the Edo period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/nabeshima-katsushige"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/nabeshima-katsushige.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Nabeshima Katsushige</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Nabeshima Katsushige was the first daimyo of the Saga domain in Hizen, Kyushu.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/nabeshima-naomasa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/nabeshima-naomasa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Nabeshima Naomasa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Nabeshima Naomasa was daimyo of the Saga domain in Kyushu from 1830 to 1861.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/nabeshima-naoshige"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/nabeshima-naoshige.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Nabeshima Naoshige</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Nabeshima Naoshige was a Japanese warlord of Hizen province in Kyushu.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-nabigha-al-dhubyani"><span class="enc-name">Al-Nabigha al-Dhubyani</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Nabigha al-Dhubyani was a pre-Islamic Arab poet of the 6th century, counted in some collections among the poets of the Mu'allaqat.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/nabis"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/nabis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Nabis</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Nabis was the last ruler of independent Sparta, from 207 to 192 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/nablus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/nablus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Nablus</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Nablus is a city in the northern West Bank between Mount Ebal and Mount Gerizim, next to the site of ancient Shechem.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/nabonassar"><span class="enc-name">Nabonassar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Nabonassar, in Akkadian Nabu-nasir, was king of Babylon from 747 to 734 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/nabongo-mumia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/nabongo-mumia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Nabongo Mumia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Nabongo Mumia was king, or nabongo, of the Wanga kingdom in western Kenya from 1882 until his death in 1949.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/nabonidus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Nabonidus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Nabonidus was the last king of the Chaldean or Neo-Babylonian Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus-chronicle"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/nabonidus-chronicle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Nabonidus Chronicle</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Nabonidus Chronicle is an ancient Babylonian cuneiform text recording the reign of Nabonidus, last king of the Chaldean Empire, and the Persian conquest of Babylon.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/nabopolassar"><span class="enc-name">Nabopolassar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Nabopolassar was a Chaldean leader who seized Babylon in 626 BCE and founded the Neo-Babylonian or Chaldean Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/nabu-apla-iddina"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/nabu-apla-iddina.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Nabu-apla-iddina</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Nabu-apla-iddina was king of Babylon from about 888 to 855 BCE, one of the longest reigns of the period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/nabu-mukin-apli"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/nabu-mukin-apli.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Nabu-mukin-apli</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Nabu-mukin-apli was king of Babylon from about 978 to 943 BCE and the first ruler of the Dynasty of E.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/nabu-mukin-zeri"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/nabu-mukin-zeri.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Nabu-mukin-zeri</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Nabu-mukin-zeri was a chief of the Chaldean tribe of Bit-Amukani who seized the throne of Babylon in 731 BCE after a period of turmoil following the death of Nabonassar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/nabu-shumu-libur"><span class="enc-name">Nabu-shumu-libur</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Nabu-shumu-libur was the last king of the Second Dynasty of Isin, ruling Babylonia from about 1033 to 1026 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/nachan-kaan"><span class="enc-name">Nachan Kaan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Nachan Kaan was the Maya lord of Chetumal, on the south-east coast of the Yucatán peninsula, at the time of the Spanish conquest.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/nachi-cocom"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/nachi-cocom.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Nachi Cocom</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Nachi Cocom was the halach uinic, or supreme lord, of t